the HDDMA and the HDCA both share vi_0;

that means that the assertion is a wire-or of both of them.
the HDCA clearing it won't necessarily clear the interrupt, since the HDDMA might also have the level up.

--------------------

every emulated port number in here is a #define, and the real cards
were strapped: the djdma's base, the hdca's, the console's, the two
pics.  a machine under this simulator should be able to be a machine
whose boards sit somewhere else, so the port numbers want to be
overridable from the command line - an option per emulation, or one
option that takes <card>:<addr> pairs.

it is not only a matter of taste: a real machine with two of these
cards, or with a board at a non-default address, is exactly the case
the driver code has to survive, and right now the simulator cannot
pose the question.

the strap options are the same shape as -j (the djdma's J2), which is
already there: name the card, say where its strap is.

--------------------

the media changer conditions which drive is being cycled on the drive
number alone.  parse_drive() in djdma.c accepts the name "djdma" as a
literal and then drops it, keeping only the 0-7, and loaded_set[] /
loaded_vol[] are indexed by that number.  that is the same shortfall as
the port numbers above: a machine with two djdma cards has no way to say
which one's drive is meant, and a medium that can be changed on some
other controller cannot be named at all.

the grammar already carries the controller - "djdma:2", sys/MEDIACHG.md
- so what it wants is for that name to resolve against the machine's
boards rather than against a string constant, and for the loaded-set
table to be keyed by controller instance and unit, not by a bare 0-7.

